00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,860
This was the first of the four questions
at the start of the module, and it's the

2
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:10,164
last one to be answered.

3
00:00:10,504 --> 00:00:15,600
Two things happened. On Wednesday morning
a colleague rebased the branch, which

4
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:20,607
replaces its commits with new copies,
and published it with git push --force.

5
00:00:20,647 --> 00:00:25,938
That removed Asha's commit from the server.
Then Asha ran git pull --rebase, which

6
00:00:25,978 --> 00:00:30,099
reported success and removed the
commit from her branch as well.

7
00:00:30,439 --> 00:00:34,665
The commit is not destroyed. It's in
exactly one place, and you'll find it.

8
00:00:34,705 --> 00:00:39,158
Where would you look first? Keep your
guess. More usefully, you'll see the same

9
00:00:39,198 --> 00:00:42,116
Wednesday morning replayed
with a push that refuses.

10
00:00:43,956 --> 00:00:47,730
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:47,770 --> 00:00:51,418
This video closes the rebase
module, and it has three jobs.

12
00:00:51,758 --> 00:00:55,904
First, publishing. After a rebase,
a plain push is always rejected.

13
00:00:55,944 --> 00:00:59,465
That rejection is not an
obstacle. It's the safety check.

14
00:00:59,505 --> 00:01:04,713
You have to overwrite the server's ref, its
name for the branch, and the only choice

15
00:01:04,753 --> 00:01:06,650
is which conditions you attach.

16
00:01:06,989 --> 00:01:11,902
A lease is one such condition: overwrite
only if the server's branch is still where

17
00:01:11,942 --> 00:01:16,139
you expect. You met it in the remotes
module from the side of the push.

18
00:01:16,179 --> 00:01:21,152
Today you meet it from the side of the
rebase, and you see what a bare --force costs

19
00:01:21,192 --> 00:01:21,928
the teammate.

20
00:01:22,268 --> 00:01:25,908
Second, the question every team
argues about: rebase or merge.

21
00:01:25,948 --> 00:01:30,776
You'll integrate the same work both ways
and compare the results object by object,

22
00:01:30,816 --> 00:01:33,150
so that the argument can be about facts.

23
00:01:33,490 --> 00:01:37,968
Third, the settings that make your chosen
habits the default, and a short pointer to

24
00:01:38,008 --> 00:01:41,733
two experimental commands that the
frontier part of the course covers.

25
00:01:43,573 --> 00:01:45,151
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:01:45,491 --> 00:01:46,903
After this video you can:

27
00:01:47,243 --> 00:01:52,375
Publish a rebased branch with a lease and
--force-if-includes, and explain each

28
00:01:52,415 --> 00:01:52,768
guard.

29
00:01:53,108 --> 00:01:57,626
Describe the damage a bare --force
does, and how the teammate recovers.

30
00:01:57,966 --> 00:02:01,154
Argue for a merge and for a
rebase of the same integration.

31
00:02:01,494 --> 00:02:03,655
Justify each rebase setting you enable.

32
00:02:03,995 --> 00:02:08,230
Say what git replay and git history
are, as pointers to Part 11.

33
00:02:10,070 --> 00:02:14,959
Publishing. In one sentence: after a rebase
the server's branch is not an ancestor

34
00:02:14,999 --> 00:02:19,888
of yours, so a normal push is refused. You
have to overwrite the server's ref, and

35
00:02:19,928 --> 00:02:23,735
the only choice is which conditions
you attach to the overwrite.

36
00:02:23,775 --> 00:02:28,664
On screen: your two new copies on top of
main, and origin/feat/ingest, your record

37
00:02:28,704 --> 00:02:31,429
of the server's branch,
still on the old pair.

38
00:02:31,769 --> 00:02:35,764
Every forced push is 🔴 DANGEROUS:
it replaces history on a remote.

39
00:02:35,804 --> 00:02:38,455
The forms differ in what
must be true first.

40
00:02:38,795 --> 00:02:42,985
The account this course owes for
a 🔴 command, from the textbook.

41
00:02:43,025 --> 00:02:47,613
A forced push changes the server's
ref, for every ref the push covers.

42
00:02:47,653 --> 00:02:53,033
It can destroy commits that exist only on
the server and in other people's clones.

43
00:02:53,073 --> 00:02:58,388
Preview with git fetch followed by git log
--oneline HEAD..origin/<branch>, which

44
00:02:58,428 --> 00:03:01,957
lists what you are about to
remove, or with --dry-run.

45
00:03:02,297 --> 00:03:05,599
Recovery depends on a clone
that still has the commits.

46
00:03:05,639 --> 00:03:09,548
And appropriate use of plain --force
and of the bare lease: none.

47
00:03:09,588 --> 00:03:14,409
The two guarded forms cost the same
keystrokes, and with them everything removed

48
00:03:14,449 --> 00:03:19,391
from the server is something your clone
has, so your own reflog, the local list of

49
00:03:19,431 --> 00:03:22,003
where your branch has
been, can restore it.

50
00:03:22,343 --> 00:03:26,921
That last sentence is the whole argument.
With the guards, you can only remove what

51
00:03:26,961 --> 00:03:27,421
you hold.

52
00:03:27,761 --> 00:03:32,300
Rebase or merge. Both integrate the same
work, and both reach the same content.

53
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:37,111
What differs is the history, and what that
history costs for bisect, the search for

54
00:03:37,151 --> 00:03:41,807
the commit that changed a behaviour, for
review, and for the people who share the

55
00:03:41,847 --> 00:03:42,212
branch.

56
00:03:42,552 --> 00:03:47,940
The textbook's verdict: neither column
wins. Three questions decide a given case.

57
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:52,630
Is the branch shared? Then merge.
What does the target branch require?

58
00:03:52,670 --> 00:03:57,790
Must every intermediate commit work?
Then a rebase needs --exec, or a squash.

59
00:03:58,130 --> 00:03:59,047
Configuration.

60
00:03:59,387 --> 00:04:04,381
Every row has had its demonstration
in this module or the ones before.

61
00:04:04,421 --> 00:04:08,840
The git config set and get
subcommands need Git 2.46 or later.

62
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:13,947
On older versions you write git config
--global rebase.autoSquash true.

63
00:04:15,787 --> 00:04:19,582
The textbook's analogy for
publishing: a compare-and-swap.

64
00:04:19,622 --> 00:04:24,873
A plain force is an unconditional write.
A lease is "set the ref to X only if it

65
00:04:24,913 --> 00:04:25,667
still is Y".

66
00:04:26,007 --> 00:04:30,404
It breaks at Y. You don't type the
expected value. Git reads it from your

67
00:04:30,444 --> 00:04:35,449
remote-tracking branch, and anything that
fetches can change that behind your back.

68
00:04:35,489 --> 00:04:40,555
The two stronger forms repair the analogy
in two different ways: --force-if-includes

69
00:04:40,595 --> 00:04:45,601
adds "and Y must be something my branch
once contained", and the explicit form lets

70
00:04:45,641 --> 00:04:46,816
you type Y yourself.

71
00:04:47,156 --> 00:04:52,106
For rebase against merge, one sentence to
keep: a merge records what happened, and a

72
00:04:52,146 --> 00:04:55,848
rebase records what you wish had
happened. Both are legitimate.

73
00:04:56,188 --> 00:04:57,374
The first is a log.

74
00:04:57,714 --> 00:05:02,678
The second is an edited account, and edited
accounts are fine as long as every page

75
00:05:02,718 --> 00:05:07,681
of them has been checked, and as long as
nobody else is holding the unedited pages.

76
00:05:09,521 --> 00:05:12,809
The same diverged pair of
branches, integrated twice.

77
00:05:12,849 --> 00:05:17,456
By merge: your three commits stay, and
one new commit joins the two lines.

78
00:05:17,496 --> 00:05:22,605
By rebase: three new copies in one line.
Look at the line under each final commit,

79
00:05:22,645 --> 00:05:24,677
and keep it in mind for the demo.

80
00:05:26,517 --> 00:05:28,296
Part 1: publishing with guards.

81
00:05:28,636 --> 00:05:32,883
Your rebased copies, and
origin/feat/ingest still on the old pair.

82
00:05:32,923 --> 00:05:33,728
A plain push:

83
00:05:34,068 --> 00:05:38,656
Rejected, "fetch first". That's the safety
check, and it's telling you something you

84
00:05:38,696 --> 00:05:41,520
didn't know: the server has
a commit you don't have.

85
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:42,292
Now the lease.

86
00:05:42,632 --> 00:05:47,610
stale info: the server has a commit that
your origin/feat/ingest does not know.

87
00:05:47,650 --> 00:05:49,007
The lease did its job.

88
00:05:49,347 --> 00:05:52,594
Now the failure mode that the
manual itself warns about.

89
00:05:52,634 --> 00:05:56,995
Something fetches for you: an editor,
a status prompt, or you out of habit.

90
00:05:57,035 --> 00:06:00,340
Predict what a dry run of the
same lease says afterwards.

91
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:00,927
I'll wait.

92
00:06:10,767 --> 00:06:13,926
The fetch moved origin/feat/ingest
to Asha's commit.

93
00:06:13,966 --> 00:06:19,033
The server and your remote-tracking branch
agree again, the lease is satisfied, and

94
00:06:19,073 --> 00:06:23,832
the dry run shows that the push would
replace c60bc13, a commit you have never

95
00:06:23,872 --> 00:06:26,601
looked at. Careful people
walk into this one.

96
00:06:26,941 --> 00:06:32,162
"Remote ref updated since checkout." c60bc13
is in no reflog entry of your branch,

97
00:06:32,202 --> 00:06:37,294
so your rewrite can't have been based on
it. The fix is to rebase what is really

98
00:06:37,334 --> 00:06:37,679
there.

99
00:06:38,019 --> 00:06:43,500
git reset --hard origin/feat/ingest 🔴, on
a clean tree, with your earlier copies

100
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:49,159
still in the reflog. Then the rebase replays
three commits, hers included, and the

101
00:06:49,199 --> 00:06:50,884
guarded push is accepted.

102
00:06:51,224 --> 00:06:55,812
The explicit form doesn't depend on
the remote-tracking branch at all.

103
00:06:55,852 --> 00:07:00,771
labs/run ch09/force-lease-explicit: you
record the commit you rebased from.

104
00:07:05,911 --> 00:07:10,928
Asha pushed, something fetched, and the
explicit lease still refuses: the server is

105
00:07:10,968 --> 00:07:12,390
not at the ID you wrote.

106
00:07:12,730 --> 00:07:15,693
And the bare lease, in the
same state, would pass.

107
00:07:16,033 --> 00:07:21,395
Part 2: what plain --force does.
labs/run ch09/force-damage.

108
00:07:21,435 --> 00:07:25,537
The same situation. The
server's branch first.

109
00:07:25,877 --> 00:07:29,387
Asha's c60bc13 is on the
server. Now the careless push.

110
00:07:29,427 --> 00:07:32,679
The five answers were given
in the Concept section.

111
00:07:32,719 --> 00:07:37,069
The answer to "when is it appropriate"
was "never, for plain force".

112
00:07:37,109 --> 00:07:40,038
The demo runs it so that
you can see the cost.

113
00:07:45,178 --> 00:07:50,405
Asha's commit is gone from the server's
branch, and the last command prints nothing.

114
00:07:50,445 --> 00:07:55,547
A bare repository, one with no working
tree, like the server's, keeps no reflog by

115
00:07:55,587 --> 00:07:59,623
default, so the server can't tell
you what the branch used to be.

116
00:07:59,663 --> 00:08:03,134
Tuesday's commit is "gone from
GitHub". Then Asha pulls.

117
00:08:03,174 --> 00:08:07,962
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds:
write the log you expect on her laptop.

118
00:08:16,502 --> 00:08:20,677
"Successfully rebased", and her branch
is identical to the new upstream.

119
00:08:20,717 --> 00:08:24,014
"Add chunker" is not in it.
Gone from her laptop as well.

120
00:08:24,054 --> 00:08:27,585
If you put her chunker on top,
that was the reasonable guess.

121
00:08:27,925 --> 00:08:33,827
The reflog of her origin/feat/ingest holds
c60bc13, "update by push", below the

122
00:08:33,867 --> 00:08:34,880
forced update.

123
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:37,230
The fork point is that commit, her own.

124
00:08:37,570 --> 00:08:41,005
So where is it? In the reflog
of her branch, and only there.

125
00:08:41,045 --> 00:08:42,164
Was that your guess?

126
00:08:42,504 --> 00:08:48,149
feat/ingest@{1}, "commit: Add chunker". git
cherry-pick 🟡 puts it on the new tip as

127
00:08:48,189 --> 00:08:51,094
82659ee, and an ordinary
push publishes it.

128
00:08:51,434 --> 00:08:55,738
Tuesday's commit is back on her branch,
with a new ID, and back on the server.

129
00:08:56,078 --> 00:09:01,083
Part 3: rebase or merge.
labs/run ch09/merge-vs-rebase.

130
00:09:01,123 --> 00:09:03,835
Integrate the same work twice.

131
00:09:12,175 --> 00:09:17,897
Quick quiz, two options. Are the two
final trees, the two final sets of files,

132
00:09:17,937 --> 00:09:21,737
equal? Option one: yes, to
the byte. Option two: no.

133
00:09:21,777 --> 00:09:22,919
Say it out loud.

134
00:09:31,759 --> 00:09:35,487
Option one. The same tree, to
the byte, and an empty diff.

135
00:09:35,527 --> 00:09:37,566
What differs is everything else.

136
00:09:37,906 --> 00:09:42,367
The merge added one new snapshot, the merge
commit, and kept your three commits with

137
00:09:42,407 --> 00:09:44,028
the trees you built and tested.

138
00:09:44,368 --> 00:09:50,130
The rebase produced three new commits. Two
of their trees, 8cc3ece and 9b070ab, are

139
00:09:50,170 --> 00:09:55,024
combinations of your early commits
with main that nobody has ever run.

140
00:09:55,364 --> 00:09:59,754
Read along first parents, the merged
history says "one feature landed" and the

141
00:09:59,794 --> 00:10:01,571
rebased one lists three changes.

142
00:10:01,911 --> 00:10:06,893
And the question of who still contains the
original commits has a different answer

143
00:10:06,933 --> 00:10:10,996
in the two results. demo/merged is
listed, and demo/rebased is not.

144
00:10:11,036 --> 00:10:15,650
And the rebased copies keep their author
times, with one new committer time.

145
00:10:15,990 --> 00:10:21,743
Part 4: a pointer. labs/run ch09/replay-pointer.
Two experimental commands do parts

146
00:10:21,783 --> 00:10:26,629
of this chapter's work without its
machinery. Chapter 14D covers them.

147
00:10:26,669 --> 00:10:32,422
git replay replays a range onto a new base
without touching the working tree or the

148
00:10:32,462 --> 00:10:35,563
index, so it also works
in a bare repository.

149
00:10:35,903 --> 00:10:41,518
feat/rerank was rebased while main stayed
checked out, and no state directory was

150
00:10:41,558 --> 00:10:47,312
involved. The price of having no working
tree is that it can't stop for a conflict:

151
00:10:47,352 --> 00:10:52,339
on a conflict it exits with status 1
and leaves the branch where it was.

152
00:10:52,379 --> 00:10:57,575
The textbook's version facts: introduced
in Git 2.44 as a server-side tool.

153
00:10:57,615 --> 00:11:03,440
It updates refs itself since 2.53. Its
--linearize option was added in Git 2.56, not

154
00:11:03,480 --> 00:11:04,068
run here.

155
00:11:04,408 --> 00:11:10,729
git history, new in Git 2.54, offers
single-purpose rewrites: reword, split and,

156
00:11:10,769 --> 00:11:15,499
since 2.55, fixup. drop was
added in Git 2.56, not run here.

157
00:11:15,539 --> 00:11:20,747
Both manual pages carry the word
EXPERIMENTAL in their first line.

158
00:11:22,587 --> 00:11:24,235
Five mistakes to watch for.

159
00:11:24,575 --> 00:11:29,276
Publishing a rebased branch with plain
--force. Root cause: the overwrite is

160
00:11:29,316 --> 00:11:34,205
unconditional, so commits that exist only
on the server and in other clones are

161
00:11:34,245 --> 00:11:37,262
removed, and a bare server
has no reflog of them.

162
00:11:37,602 --> 00:11:41,512
Trusting the bare lease in an environment
that fetches in the background.

163
00:11:41,552 --> 00:11:45,894
Root cause: the expected value is read
from the remote-tracking branch, which any

164
00:11:45,934 --> 00:11:46,760
fetch refreshes.

165
00:11:47,100 --> 00:11:51,088
Reading the push rejection after
a rebase as an error to get past.

166
00:11:51,128 --> 00:11:56,153
Root cause: the rejection is the fast-forward
check doing its job; here it revealed

167
00:11:56,193 --> 00:11:57,740
a commit you had not seen.

168
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:02,804
Arguing "rebase gives a clean history"
without testing the intermediate commits.

169
00:12:02,844 --> 00:12:07,806
Root cause: each replayed commit is a new
snapshot that nobody ran; a linear history

170
00:12:07,846 --> 00:12:10,248
helps bisect only if every commit builds.

171
00:12:10,588 --> 00:12:14,707
Rebasing a shared branch because
the team "prefers linear history".

172
00:12:14,747 --> 00:12:19,672
Root cause: on a branch others use, a
rebase needs a forced push and everybody's

173
00:12:19,712 --> 00:12:22,465
cooperation, while a
merge only adds commits.

174
00:12:24,305 --> 00:12:28,918
Now, out of the lab. Here's a rule that a
backend team for LLM serving could write

175
00:12:28,958 --> 00:12:32,605
after a Wednesday morning like
that one, built from this chapter.

176
00:12:32,945 --> 00:12:38,650
One: rebase in private. On the branch of
your own pull request, rebase as you like,

177
00:12:38,690 --> 00:12:42,041
with --exec when the history
is going to be kept.

178
00:12:42,381 --> 00:12:47,878
Two: publish with both guards. git config
set --global push.useForceIfIncludes true

179
00:12:47,918 --> 00:12:53,282
adds the second check to every lease. The
textbook notes that editors and prompts

180
00:12:53,322 --> 00:12:58,218
that fetch in the background are the
usual way the bare lease is defeated.

181
00:12:58,258 --> 00:13:01,554
The manual's own example
is a scheduled git fetch.

182
00:13:01,894 --> 00:13:04,844
Three: on a branch somebody
else pushes to, merge.

183
00:13:05,184 --> 00:13:09,457
Four: let the pull request's merge
method decide what lands on main.

184
00:13:09,797 --> 00:13:14,066
Two platform facts from the textbook,
described from the documentation.

185
00:13:14,106 --> 00:13:19,103
A "Require linear history" rule on GitHub
blocks merge commits, which leaves squash

186
00:13:19,143 --> 00:13:24,018
or rebase. And after an accepted forced
push, pull requests for the branch follow

187
00:13:24,058 --> 00:13:29,054
it. Where the rules dismiss stale approvals,
an approval is dismissed when the diff

188
00:13:29,094 --> 00:13:33,788
changes. The interface changes. The linked
documentation in the chapter is the

189
00:13:33,828 --> 00:13:34,395
reference.

190
00:13:36,235 --> 00:13:42,830
Your turn. Do Exercise 9.9, Level 4, "the
morning rebase that replays somebody

191
00:13:42,870 --> 00:13:47,338
else's commits", in
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

192
00:13:47,678 --> 00:13:49,966
Predict before each
state-changing command:

193
00:13:50,306 --> 00:13:54,467
The list of commits your rebase is about
to replay, and whether each one is yours.

194
00:13:54,807 --> 00:13:58,710
After the rebase: what a plain push
will say, and why that is useful.

195
00:13:59,050 --> 00:14:03,962
For each of the three forced forms: accepted
or rejected in this state, and on what

196
00:14:04,002 --> 00:14:04,619
comparison.

197
00:14:04,959 --> 00:14:09,862
The challenge is Incident 5,
incidents/05-rebased-shared-branch.

198
00:14:09,902 --> 00:14:12,025
Read the symptoms file only.

199
00:14:13,865 --> 00:14:18,856
Q156: "Compare --force,
--force-with-lease, and --force-with-lease

200
00:14:18,896 --> 00:14:25,182
--force-if-includes. Describe a sequence
of events in which the second overwrites a

201
00:14:25,222 --> 00:14:28,383
colleague's commit and the third refuses."

202
00:14:30,723 --> 00:14:35,373
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
states for each of the three the condition

203
00:14:35,413 --> 00:14:40,178
under which the server's ref is overwritten,
in terms of specific refs and reflogs.

204
00:14:40,218 --> 00:14:44,984
Then it tells the sequence as a timeline
with three actors, you, the server and the

205
00:14:45,024 --> 00:14:49,789
colleague, and marks the one step at which
the second form's condition becomes true

206
00:14:49,829 --> 00:14:51,758
without your having seen anything.

207
00:14:52,098 --> 00:14:56,615
It says exactly what the third form checks
at that moment and why the check fails.

208
00:14:56,655 --> 00:15:00,060
Close with the setting that makes
the third form your default.

209
00:15:01,900 --> 00:15:06,028
Let's land this. You should now be
able to say, in your own words:

210
00:15:06,368 --> 00:15:11,649
After a rebase a plain push is rejected,
and that rejection is the safety check.

211
00:15:11,689 --> 00:15:17,237
I publish with --force-with-lease
--force-if-includes, or with an explicit lease, so

212
00:15:17,277 --> 00:15:21,428
that I can only remove from the
server what my own clone holds.

213
00:15:21,468 --> 00:15:27,016
Plain --force and the bare lease have no
appropriate use on a branch others push to.

214
00:15:27,056 --> 00:15:32,138
A commit that a forced push removed, and
that a teammate's pull --rebase then

215
00:15:32,178 --> 00:15:35,929
dropped, is still in the reflog
of the teammate's branch.

216
00:15:36,269 --> 00:15:41,454
Merge and rebase reach the same tree. The
merge keeps the tested commits and records

217
00:15:41,494 --> 00:15:45,808
the integration. The rebase gives
one line of new, untested snapshots.

218
00:15:45,848 --> 00:15:50,909
On a shared branch I merge. I enable each
rebase setting for a reason I can state.

219
00:15:52,749 --> 00:15:59,800
Read sections 9.17 to 9.23 of Chapter
9 and do the Practice section 9.25.

220
00:16:00,140 --> 00:16:05,325
That closes the rebase module. You can
rewrite a branch, publish it safely, and find

221
00:16:05,365 --> 00:16:08,622
your way back. Do the exercise
before the next video.

222
00:16:08,662 --> 00:16:13,349
Next time: what cherry-pick does, a
three-way merge that makes a new commit.

223
00:16:13,389 --> 00:16:16,584
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

224
00:16:16,624 --> 00:16:18,077
See you in the next one.
